= Biological sequence =

== Overview ==
A '''biological sequence''' is an ordered representation of biological monomers or states, most commonly nucleotide sequences for DNA/RNA and amino-acid sequences for proteins.

Sequence data are typically represented symbolically using alphabets such as A, C, G and T/U for nucleic acids or amino-acid codes for proteins. The symbolic record represents an inferred or measured molecular sequence rather than being the physical molecule itself.

Sequences can contain ambiguity codes, gaps, masked positions, quality information, coordinates, identifiers, and metadata. Reference sequences and sample-derived sequences can differ because of biological variation, sequencing error, assembly, or representation choices.

Biological sequence is foundational to alignment, database search, annotation, genomics, and phylogenetics, but sequence alone does not determine biological function.

== Boundaries ==
* biological sequence ≠ physical molecule
* sequence ≠ sequence annotation
* sequence similarity ≠ functional identity

